---
Part VI, Chapter 5: The language server
Package: packages/loom-lang/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The language server

The Loom language server is an Effect program. `startLanguageServer` runs it: it builds the pipeline layers once and captures a warm runtime, because Volar's hooks are synchronous and the language plugin runs the projection on that runtime. On `initialize` the server finds a TypeScript compiler to check against and collects the plugins of the workspace the editor opened. From then on it serves every loom with the plugins of the loom's own workspace. It collects them the first time it meets a loom there, and again whenever that workspace's configuration changes. It also rereads a chapter whenever the chapter changes on disk while the editor has it closed, and whenever the editor closes it without saving, so an open loom never answers from text the chapter no longer holds.

Any editor that speaks the Language Server Protocol can start the server. VS Code starts it through the Loom extension, which loads a thin entry module. Neovim, Helix, Zed, and the JetBrains IDEs start it with the `loom lsp` command and talk to it over standard input and output. So the server lives in the CLI, the one tool every Loom user already has.

## Imports

The server is built from Volar's language-server toolkit over a `ManagedRuntime`. From the toolkit it takes the connection, the TypeScript project factory, `loadTsdkByPath`, and the types of a server, a project, a service plugin, a file the editor reports changed, and the language whose scripts a project projects. `FileChangeType` says whether a reported file was created, changed, or deleted. It imports `LoomCompiler`, the projector and cache the runtime warms, and `LoomFormatter`, which formats a file on it, with the `DocumentSource`, `PackageConfig`, and `LoomConfig` they require, and the two halves this module registers, `loomLanguagePlugin` and `loomServicePlugins`, and `loomsIn`, which lists the looms a project holds under a folder. From the language store come `workspaceOf` and `workspaceRoot`, which find the workspace a path belongs to, and what the server needs to keep a TypeScript in a workspace's store: `storeDir`, which locates the store, `storeTypeScript`, which finds a TypeScript there, `installTypeScript` and its `typeScriptRange`, which put one there, and `standalone`, which says whether Loom runs as the standalone executable.

Effect's `Schema` reads the editor's initialization options, `Option` carries a compiler or a workspace that may not be there, and `Data` types the failure when no compiler is. `Cache` holds what the server builds for each workspace, and `Array` gathers across them. `Semaphore` holds back a request while the server reprojects the open looms, and `Ref` holds the looms the editor has changed since the last request. A `Source` is what the compiler reads a loom's text through, and `ReadError` is its failure. Node's `createRequire` finds an installed `typescript` package the way Node itself would, and `readFileSync` reads a loom's saved text, for a loom the editor closes and for one it does not hold. `join` builds the path a search starts from, and `dirname` trims a file's path to the directory that holds it. `URI` is the address Volar names a document by, and it parses the addresses the editor reports.

=>

import {
  Array,
  Cache,
  Data,
  Effect,
  Layer,
  ManagedRuntime,
  Option,
  Schema,
  Ref,
  Semaphore,
  pipe,
} from 'effect'
import {
  createConnection,
  createServer,
  createTypeScriptProject,
  FileChangeType,
  type FileEvent,
  type Language,
  type LanguageServer,
  type LanguageServerProject,
  type LanguageServicePlugin,
  loadTsdkByPath,
} from '@volar/language-server/node'
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'
import { LoomCompiler, DocumentSource } from './LoomCompiler'
import { ReadError, type Source } from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'
import { LoomFormatter } from './LoomFormatter'
import { PackageConfig } from './PackageConfig'
import { LoomConfig } from '@athrio/loom-config/LoomConfig'
import {
  installTypeScript,
  standalone,
  storeDir,
  storeTypeScript,
  typeScriptRange,
  workspaceOf,
  workspaceRoot,
} from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LoomStore'
import { loomLanguagePlugin, loomServicePlugins } from './LoomLanguagePlugin'
import { loomsIn } from './LoomProject'

## A TypeScript to check against

The server type-checks every TypeScript section against a real compiler, so it needs one before it can start. Volar's `loadTsdkByPath` loads the compiler from a TypeScript SDK directory, which Volar calls the tsdk: the `lib` directory of a `typescript` package, where `typescript.js` sits beside the standard library's declaration files. The server's first task is to find that directory.

VS Code supplies it. The Loom extension sends the path of the TypeScript that VS Code ships, in the `typescript.tsdk` field of its initialization options. `EditorTsdkSchema` is the shape of that field, and `editorTsdk` decodes it from whatever the client sent, yielding nothing when the field is absent or malformed.

=>

const EditorTsdkSchema = Schema.Struct({
  typescript: Schema.Struct({ tsdk: Schema.String }),
})

const editorTsdk = (options: unknown): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.map(
    Schema.decodeUnknownOption(EditorTsdkSchema)(options),
    (decoded) => decoded.typescript.tsdk,
  )

~

Every other editor starts `loom lsp` with no such option, so the server looks for the compiler itself. What it looks for is TypeScript's JavaScript API, the `lib/typescript.js` file that TypeScript 6 and every earlier release ship. TypeScript 7 is a native program, and its package holds no such file for Volar to load.

`installedTsdk` resolves `typescript/lib/typescript.js` the way Node's `require` would from a given file, walking up its `node_modules` directories, and returns the directory that holds it. It yields nothing when the walk finds no TypeScript, and nothing when the TypeScript it finds is version 7, so the search moves on to the next place.

=>

const installedTsdk = (from: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.map(
    Option.liftThrowable(() => createRequire(from).resolve('typescript/lib/typescript.js'))(),
    dirname,
  )

~

`foundTsdk` tries four places in order and takes the first that holds a compiler. The editor's own path comes first, so VS Code keeps the TypeScript it ships. The project being edited comes second, so a section is checked by the project's own TypeScript whenever it is one the server can load.

Loom's own install comes third: the directories above the server's module, which hold `typescript` when Loom is installed beside it. Only the ES-module bundle that `loom lsp` runs knows its own location. The CommonJS bundles, the VS Code extension's `server.js` and the `loom-language-server` bin, compile `import.meta` to an empty object, so the search finds nothing there, and VS Code never needs it. The standalone executable skips this place, since nothing is installed beside it. Its modules live inside the executable, and a search that starts there would walk up from the working directory instead.

The workspace's store comes last. It holds a TypeScript when Loom has already installed one there, for this server or for `loom format`.

=>

const besideLoom = (): Option.Option<string> =>
  standalone ? Option.none() : installedTsdk(import.meta.url)

const foundTsdk = (options: unknown, root: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    editorTsdk(options),
    Option.orElse(() => installedTsdk(join(root, 'package.json'))),
    Option.orElse(besideLoom),
    Option.orElse(() => Option.map(storeTypeScript(root), dirname)),
  )

~

A project written in another language holds no TypeScript, and nothing sits beside the standalone executable, so outside VS Code all four places can be empty. The server then puts TypeScript 6 into the workspace's store itself, through the store's `installTypeScript`, and loads it from there. The standalone executable unpacks the copy it carries, with no network at all. The npm package downloads one, which needs the network that once. Either way it happens once per workspace, and every later start finds the copy in the store.

The install needs a workspace to hold the store. A folder that lies in no workspace has no store, and installing one would turn the folder into a workspace behind the person's back, so the server installs nothing there.

When the server finds no compiler and can install none, it refuses to start and says why. `MissingTypeScript` names the folder it searched and says which TypeScript it can load. It says why no install happened, either because the folder lies in no workspace or because the install failed, and then it gives the ways to supply a compiler. The error travels back to the editor as the answer to `initialize`, so the person reads the reason instead of watching a server die.

=>

export class MissingTypeScript extends Data.TaggedError('MissingTypeScript')<{
  readonly root: string
  readonly failedInstall: Option.Option<string>
}> {
  get message(): string {
    const why = Option.match(this.failedInstall, {
      onNone: () =>
        `${this.root} lies in no Loom workspace, so there is no store to install one into; run \`loom init\` there`,
      onSome: (cause) => `installing one into the workspace's store failed: ${cause}`,
    })
    return `Loom's language server needs TypeScript 6 or earlier, and found none in ${this.root} or beside Loom. TypeScript 7 has no JavaScript API for it to load. ${why}. You can also install one in the project with \`bun add -d typescript@6\`, or have the editor send its path as \`initializationOptions.typescript.tsdk\`.`
  }
}

const fetchedTsdk = (root: string): Effect.Effect<string, MissingTypeScript> =>
  Option.match(workspaceOf(root), {
    onNone: () => Effect.fail(new MissingTypeScript({ root, failedInstall: Option.none() })),
    onSome: (workspace) =>
      Effect.logInfo(`installing TypeScript ${typeScriptRange} into ${storeDir(workspace)}`).pipe(
        Effect.andThen(installTypeScript(workspace)),
        Effect.map(dirname),
        Effect.mapError(
          (error) =>
            new MissingTypeScript({ root, failedInstall: Option.some(String(error.cause)) }),
        ),
      ),
  })

const tsdkFor = (options: unknown, root: string): Effect.Effect<string, MissingTypeScript> =>
  Option.match(foundTsdk(options, root), {
    onNone: () => fetchedTsdk(root),
    onSome: Effect.succeed,
  })

## Every loom answers to its own workspace

A workspace decides which languages its looms use and where their services come from. Its `config.yaml` names the active languages, and its store under `.loom/services/` holds the packages that serve them. Workspaces can nest. A Git worktree checked out inside a repository carries a `.loom/config.yaml` of its own. An editor opens one folder, but the looms it shows may belong to several workspaces.

So the server serves each loom with the services of the workspace the loom belongs to, not those of the folder the editor opened. A loom in a nested workspace is checked and formatted with that workspace's languages, at the versions its own store holds. `loom format` loads the same services for the same loom. Whether the editor opened the outer folder or the nested one makes no difference to the loom.

`workspaceFor` names the workspace a document belongs to. For a file on disk it is the nearest directory above the file that holds a `.loom/config.yaml`, the one `workspaceOf` finds. A document outside every workspace, or one that is not a file on disk, belongs to `home`. That is the workspace of the folder the editor opened, or the folder itself when it lies in no workspace.

=>

const workspaceFor = (home: string, uri: URI): string =>
  uri.scheme === 'file' ? Option.getOrElse(workspaceOf(dirname(uri.fsPath)), () => home) : home

~

Volar already has a seam for this. For every request, Volar asks a project which language service answers the document. Every language service carries its own list of service plugins, and Volar's TypeScript project builds its language services from the plugins of the server it is set up with. So `projectOver` builds a TypeScript project for one workspace and sets it up with a copy of the server whose `languageServicePlugins` are that workspace's. Every language service the project builds then carries the workspace's plugins. The project's looms still project through the Loom language plugin, on the one warm runtime every workspace shares.

=>

type Tsdk = ReturnType<typeof loadTsdkByPath>

const projectOver = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  tsdk: Tsdk,
  plugins: ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>,
): LanguageServerProject => {
  const project = createTypeScriptProject(tsdk.typescript, tsdk.diagnosticMessages, () => ({
    languagePlugins: [loomLanguagePlugin(runtime)],
  }))
  project.setup({ ...server, languageServicePlugins: [...plugins] })
  return project
}

~

`workspaceProject` is the project the server hands to Volar. Its `projects` cache holds one TypeScript project per workspace. Its `services` cache holds the plugins each project is built over. `getLanguageService` answers a document from the project of the document's workspace. It first takes the `reprojecting` semaphore, waiting while a reprojection holds it, and settles the edits the editor has made since the last request. `getExistingLanguageServices` settles them too, then reaches every project the `projects` cache holds, as `reload` does. Volar sets the project up with the server once the editor has initialized. The `projects` cache already builds every workspace's project with that same server, so `setup` has two tasks left. It starts `watchConfigs`, which keeps both caches and the builds of each workspace's looms in step with its configuration, and `watchLooms`, which keeps every open loom in step with the closed chapters it reads.

=>

export const workspaceProject = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  services: Cache.Cache<string, ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  reprojecting: Semaphore.Semaphore,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): LanguageServerProject => ({
  setup: (server) => {
    watchConfigs(server, home, services, projects, edited)
    watchLooms(server, home, projects, reprojecting, edited)
  },
  getLanguageService: (uri) =>
    runtime.runPromise(
      reprojecting.withPermits(1)(settleEdits(server, home, projects, edited)).pipe(
        Effect.andThen(Cache.get(projects, workspaceFor(home, uri))),
        Effect.flatMap((project) => Effect.promise(async () => project.getLanguageService(uri))),
      ),
    ),
  getExistingLanguageServices: () =>
    runtime.runPromise(
      reprojecting.withPermits(1)(settleEdits(server, home, projects, edited)).pipe(
        Effect.andThen(Cache.values(projects)),
        Effect.flatMap((all) =>
          Effect.forEach(all, (project) =>
            Effect.promise(async () => (await project.getExistingLanguageServices()) ?? []),
          ),
        ),
        Effect.map(Array.flatten),
      ),
    ),
  reload: () =>
    runtime.runSync(
      Cache.values(projects).pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((all) =>
          Effect.forEach(all, (project) => Effect.sync(() => project.reload()), { discard: true }),
        ),
      ),
    ),
})

~

A workspace's configuration can change while the server runs. `loom add` installs a language's service in the store and then rewrites `config.yaml`. `loom remove` takes the service out of the store and rewrites `config.yaml` too. So a change to `config.yaml` is the one sign that a workspace's services changed. `watchConfigs` asks the editor to watch every `.loom/config.yaml` and listens for the changes the editor reports. `reconfiguredWorkspaces` reads a batch of changed files and returns the workspaces whose configuration changed, each the folder that holds a changed `.loom/` folder.

`forgetWorkspace` drops what the server built for one workspace. It reloads the workspace's TypeScript project, which disposes the project's language services and the plugins inside them, then removes the workspace from both caches. The next request for one of the workspace's looms collects its plugins afresh, from the configuration and the store as they now stand, and builds a new project over them. Under Node, a service `loom add` replaced comes back at its new version, because the loader imports each version at an address of its own.

A configuration also decides how each of the workspace's looms is built. It names the anchor delimiters a loom uses, its primary language, and the variables its value warps read. The compiler's build cache holds every loom it built under the old configuration, and the cache would hand those builds back unchanged. So `rebuildUnder` evicts every build within the workspace through the compiler's `invalidateWithin`. A closed loom is built again from disk the next time a composition reads it. An open loom may hold unsaved edits, and a composition that missed it in the cache would read it from disk. So `rebuildUnder` also notes every open loom of the workspace as edited, and the next request rebuilds each one from the editor's text before anything reads it. The build cache on disk needs nothing, because it keys each build by the settings it was built under.

`reconfigure` rebuilds and forgets every workspace a batch of changes names. It then asks Volar to refresh, clearing the diagnostics on screen, so every open loom is checked again with the services it now has. A batch that names no `config.yaml`, such as a change to a `tsconfig.json`, forgets nothing and leaves the refresh to Volar's own projects.

=>

const reconfiguredWorkspaces = (changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  pipe(
    changes,
    Array.filter((change) => change.uri.endsWith('/.loom/config.yaml')),
    Array.map((change) => dirname(dirname(fileURLToPath(change.uri)))),
    Array.dedupe,
  )

const forgetWorkspace = (
  services: Cache.Cache<string, ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  workspace: string,
): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Cache.getSuccess(projects, workspace).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(
      Option.match({
        onNone: () => Effect.void,
        onSome: (project) => Effect.sync(() => project.reload()),
      }),
    ),
    Effect.andThen(Cache.invalidate(projects, workspace)),
    Effect.andThen(Cache.invalidate(services, workspace)),
  )

const rebuildUnder = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
  workspace: string,
): Effect.Effect<void, never, LoomCompiler> =>
  LoomCompiler.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((compiler) => compiler.invalidateWithin(workspace)),
    Effect.andThen(
      Effect.forEach(openLoomsIn(server, home, workspace), (uri) => recordEdit(edited, uri), {
        discard: true,
      }),
    ),
  )

const reconfigure = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  services: Cache.Cache<string, ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
  workspaces: ReadonlyArray<string>,
): Effect.Effect<void, never, LoomCompiler> =>
  workspaces.length === 0
    ? Effect.void
    : Effect.forEach(
        workspaces,
        (workspace) =>
          rebuildUnder(server, home, edited, workspace).pipe(
            Effect.andThen(forgetWorkspace(services, projects, workspace)),
          ),
        { discard: true },
      ).pipe(Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => void server.languageFeatures.requestRefresh(true))))

const watchConfigs = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  services: Cache.Cache<string, ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): void => {
  void server.fileWatcher.watchFiles(['**/.loom/config.yaml'])
  server.fileWatcher.onDidChangeWatchedFiles(({ changes }) =>
    runtime.runSync(
      reconfigure(server, home, services, projects, edited, reconfiguredWorkspaces(changes)),
    ),
  )
}

~

One limit remains, and it concerns capabilities. The editor learns the server's capabilities once, from the answer to `initialize`, and Volar derives that answer from the plugins of `home`. A nested workspace's plugins receive only the requests those capabilities let the editor send. So do the plugins of a language activated after the server started. The Loom language is active in every workspace, and its TypeScript plugins already claim the common features: diagnostics, hover, completion, navigation, rename, and formatting. So the limit reaches only a feature that no plugin of `home` claimed at `initialize`.

## A chapter changes behind the editor's back

The editor reports every change to a loom it has open, keystroke by keystroke. A chapter the editor has closed changes too, and nothing the editor does reports it. `git pull` and `git checkout` rewrite chapters, an agent edits them, and `loom toc` rewrites the book. An open loom still reads those chapters. Its anchors resolve against the sections a closed chapter holds, and its code imports the files a closed chapter tangles.

The compiler's build cache keeps every chapter the server has read, the closed ones included. Left alone, the server would answer from the text a closed chapter held when it first read it. An open loom's code would be checked against the types a closed chapter used to export, and go-to-definition would land where a section used to be.

So `watchLooms` asks the editor to watch every `.loom` file, as `watchConfigs` watches every `config.yaml`. `isLoom` tells a loom's address by its path. `closedLoomChanges` reads a batch of reported changes and keeps those to looms the editor does not have open. The editor's own text governs an open loom, so a change on disk to an open loom tells the server nothing.

`rereadLoom` evicts one changed loom from the build cache with the compiler's `invalidate`, so the next composition reads the loom from disk again. It then decides whether the change can reach an open loom. A changed loom the cache holds can, since some composition read it. A changed loom the cache does not hold cannot, since no composition holds its old text. A new loom can, because the next composition finds it when it lists the corpus, and an anchor that named nothing may now resolve. A deleted loom always counts, because Volar may still hold its script. `rereadLoom` returns the change when it counts, and nothing otherwise. It names a loom by the same path the language plugin gives it, the `fsPath` of the loom's address, so both evict the same entry of the build cache.

=>

const isLoom = (uri: URI): boolean => uri.path.endsWith('.loom')

const closedLoomChanges = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
): ReadonlyArray<FileEvent> =>
  Array.filter(changes, (change) => {
    const uri = URI.parse(change.uri)
    return isLoom(uri) && server.documents.get(uri) === undefined
  })

const rereadLoom = (
  compiler: LoomCompiler['Service'],
  change: FileEvent,
): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<FileEvent>> =>
  compiler
    .invalidate(URI.parse(change.uri).fsPath)
    .pipe(
      Effect.map((reprojected) =>
        change.type === FileChangeType.Changed && reprojected.length === 0
          ? Option.none()
          : Option.some(change),
      ),
    )

const rereadLooms = (
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>, never, LoomCompiler> =>
  LoomCompiler.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((compiler) => Effect.forEach(changes, (change) => rereadLoom(compiler, change))),
    Effect.map(Array.getSomes),
  )

~

`rereadLooms` rereads every loom in a batch and keeps the changes that count.

Evicting a chapter is not enough for the looms the editor has open. Each open loom holds a projection, built when the loom last changed, with the sections it drew in from other chapters already inlined. Volar also keeps the diagnostics a service reports for a projection until the projection changes, whenever the service declares that its diagnostics read no other file. The TypeScript service declares exactly that. So every open loom a change can reach must be projected again.

Volar has a flag for this. A script whose `isAssociationDirty` is set is projected again on the next request for it, through the language plugin's `updateVirtualCode`, from the text the editor holds. Volar sets the flag itself when a script a loom depends on changes, but only for a loom that draws the script in by an anchor. A loom that imports a closed chapter's file through its code holds no such link, so the server marks every open loom of the workspace instead. `markStale` sets the flag on one open loom's script. The reprojection keeps the loom's own build, because the loom's text has not changed, so an unsaved edit survives it. Only the evicted chapter is read from disk again.

A workspace's project can build more than one language service. Volar's TypeScript project builds one for the `tsconfig.json` nearest a loom even when another language service answers the loom. Reading a loom's script from a language service creates the script there if it is missing, and projects the loom a second time. So `markStale` reaches each open loom through the one language service the project answers it from.

A deleted chapter leaves its script behind in Volar, with the projection it last held, and no request asks for that script again. `forgetScript` deletes it from every language service the project has built, since any of them may hold it. Deleting a script that is not there does nothing.

`refreshProject` brings one workspace's project up to date. It forgets the script of each loom it is handed, then marks every open loom of the workspace stale. `openLoomsIn` lists those open looms. A step that fails for one loom logs its cause through `logged`, and the pass moves on to the next. `reprojectWorkspace` refreshes the project of one workspace. A workspace whose project the server has not built yet holds no projections, so it needs nothing.

=>

const openLoomsIn = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  workspace: string,
): ReadonlyArray<URI> =>
  pipe(
    server.documents.all(),
    Array.map((document) => URI.parse(document.uri)),
    Array.filter((uri) => isLoom(uri) && workspaceFor(home, uri) === workspace),
  )

const logged = (step: string, uri: URI, effect: Effect.Effect<void>): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  effect.pipe(
    Effect.catchCause((cause) => Effect.logError(`loom: ${step} ${uri.fsPath} failed`, cause)),
  )

const markStale = (project: LanguageServerProject, uri: URI): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.promise(async () => project.getLanguageService(uri)).pipe(
    Effect.map((languageService) => languageService.context.language.scripts.get(uri)),
    Effect.flatMap((script) =>
      Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(script), {
        onNone: () => Effect.void,
        onSome: (stale) =>
          Effect.sync(() => {
            stale.isAssociationDirty = true
          }),
      }),
    ),
  )

const forgetScript = (language: Language<URI>, uri: URI): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.sync(() => language.scripts.delete(uri))

export const refreshProject = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  workspace: string,
  project: LanguageServerProject,
  forgotten: ReadonlyArray<URI>,
): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const languageServices = yield* Effect.promise(
      async () => (await project.getExistingLanguageServices()) ?? [],
    )
    yield* Effect.forEach(
      Array.cartesian(languageServices, forgotten),
      ([languageService, uri]) =>
        logged('forgetting', uri, forgetScript(languageService.context.language, uri)),
      { discard: true },
    )
    yield* Effect.forEach(
      openLoomsIn(server, home, workspace),
      (uri) => logged('reprojecting', uri, markStale(project, uri)),
      { discard: true },
    )
  })

const reprojectWorkspace = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  workspace: string,
  forgotten: ReadonlyArray<URI>,
): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Cache.getSuccess(projects, workspace).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(
      Option.match({
        onNone: () => Effect.void,
        onSome: (project) => refreshProject(server, home, workspace, project, forgotten),
      }),
    ),
  )

~

`reproject` brings up to date the project of every workspace the counted changes belong to. It forgets the script of every deleted loom, and of any other loom the caller names. Then it asks Volar to refresh, so the editor asks again for every open loom's diagnostics. A batch with no change that counts leaves every projection as it is.

=>

const reproject = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
  alsoForgotten: ReadonlyArray<URI>,
): Effect.Effect<void> => {
  const forgotten = [
    ...pipe(
      changes,
      Array.filter((change) => change.type === FileChangeType.Deleted),
      Array.map((change) => URI.parse(change.uri)),
    ),
    ...alsoForgotten,
  ]
  return changes.length === 0
    ? Effect.void
    : Effect.forEach(
        Array.dedupe(Array.map(changes, (change) => workspaceFor(home, URI.parse(change.uri)))),
        (workspace) => reprojectWorkspace(server, home, projects, workspace, forgotten),
        { discard: true },
      ).pipe(
        Effect.andThen(Effect.sync(() => void server.languageFeatures.requestRefresh(false))),
      )
}

~

A loom the editor closes can leave the build cache behind it too. While the loom was open, the cache held the text the loom had in the editor. When the editor closes it without saving, the file on disk still holds the text it had before. Nothing on disk changed, so the editor reports nothing. Yet every open loom that draws the closed one in now draws in text that exists nowhere.

`closeOf` compares the text the editor held with the file on disk, and turns a close into the change it amounts to. A loom closed with its text saved amounts to no change at all, and the server does nothing. A loom closed with unsaved text amounts to a change on disk. The server rereads it, and forgets the script Volar built from the unsaved text. A loom whose file is gone, because it was deleted while the editor held it open, amounts to a deletion.

=>

export const closeOf = (uri: URI, text: string): ReadonlyArray<FileEvent> =>
  Option.match(
    Option.liftThrowable(() => readFileSync(uri.fsPath, 'utf8'))(),
    {
      onNone: () => [{ uri: uri.toString(), type: FileChangeType.Deleted }],
      onSome: (saved) =>
        saved === text ? [] : [{ uri: uri.toString(), type: FileChangeType.Changed }],
    },
  )

~

`rereadOnDisk` runs the whole pass, in two halves. The rereading half runs synchronously, on the warm runtime, while the server handles the editor's notification. So every eviction finishes before the server reads the editor's next message, however large the batch.

The reprojection half asks a project for its language services, which answers with a promise, so it cannot run synchronously. `rereadOnDisk` forks it on the same runtime, holding the `reprojecting` semaphore while it runs. A forked fiber starts at once, so the reprojection takes the semaphore before the handler returns, and so before the server reads another request. The project the server hands to Volar waits on that semaphore before it answers any request. So no request is answered from an open loom's old projection between the eviction and the mark. A reprojection that fails logs its cause, so a failure never passes silently.

`watchLooms` runs `rereadOnDisk` on every batch the editor reports, and on every loom the editor closes. It also notes every loom the editor opens or changes, for the next request to settle.

=>

const rereadOnDisk = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  reprojecting: Semaphore.Semaphore,
  changes: ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>,
  alsoForgotten: ReadonlyArray<URI>,
): void => {
  const counted = runtime.runSync(rereadLooms(changes))
  runtime.runFork(
    reprojecting.withPermits(1)(
      reproject(server, home, projects, counted, alsoForgotten).pipe(
        Effect.catchCause((cause) => Effect.logError('loom: reprojecting open looms failed', cause)),
      ),
    ),
  )
}

const watchLooms = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  reprojecting: Semaphore.Semaphore,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): void => {
  void server.fileWatcher.watchFiles(['**/*.loom'])
  server.documents.onDidChangeContent(({ document }) =>
    runtime.runSync(recordEdit(edited, URI.parse(document.uri))),
  )
  server.fileWatcher.onDidChangeWatchedFiles(({ changes }) =>
    rereadOnDisk(server, home, projects, reprojecting, closedLoomChanges(server, changes), []),
  )
  server.documents.onDidClose(({ document }) => {
    const uri = URI.parse(document.uri)
    const changes = isLoom(uri) ? closeOf(uri, document.getText()) : []
    rereadOnDisk(server, home, projects, reprojecting, changes, changes.length > 0 ? [uri] : [])
  })
}

## An edit reaches the other open chapters

A chapter the editor has open changes on every keystroke, and other open chapters read it. One open chapter draws a section of another in by an anchor, or imports a file the other tangles. A rename in the editor edits both at once: the section's title in one chapter, and the anchor that names it in the other. Until the edits are saved, the text the editor holds is the only place the new names exist.

The compiler builds a chapter from the editor's text only when the editor asks about that chapter. Volar asks about the chapter a request names, and about the chapters it draws in by anchors it has already resolved. So a chapter the person is not looking at can keep the build of its text from before an edit. Another chapter that reads it then reads the old text. After a rename across two chapters, the chapter with the anchor names a section its neighbour no longer holds, and the server reports the anchor as unresolved until the neighbour is asked about too.

So the server rebuilds every edited chapter before it answers any request. `watchLooms` notes each loom the editor opens or changes in the `edited` set, through `recordEdit`. Noting an edit is all a keystroke costs. The rebuilding waits for the next request, so a burst of keystrokes costs one rebuild per chapter.

`openSource` reads a loom the way the editor shows it. It reads an open loom's text from the editor and any other loom from disk. `rebuildEdited` takes the `edited` set, leaving it empty, and rebuilds each loom in it from `openSource` through the compiler's `rebuild`. It keeps a change for each loom whose face changed, the part of a module other files can see.

=>

const openSource = (server: LanguageServer): Source => ({
  read: (path) =>
    Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(server.documents.get(URI.file(path))), {
      onSome: (document) => Effect.succeed(document.getText()),
      onNone: () =>
        Effect.try({
          try: () => readFileSync(path, 'utf8'),
          catch: (cause) => new ReadError({ path, cause }),
        }),
    }),
  list: Option.none(),
})

const recordEdit = (edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>, uri: URI): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  isLoom(uri) ? Ref.update(edited, (paths) => new Set([...paths, uri.fsPath])) : Effect.void

const rebuildEdited = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FileEvent>, never, LoomCompiler> =>
  Effect.all([Ref.getAndSet(edited, new Set<string>()), LoomCompiler]).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(([paths, compiler]) =>
      Effect.forEach(paths, (path) =>
        compiler
          .rebuild(openSource(server), path)
          .pipe(
            Effect.map((changed) =>
              changed
                ? Option.some<FileEvent>({
                    uri: URI.file(path).toString(),
                    type: FileChangeType.Changed,
                  })
                : Option.none(),
            ),
          ),
      ),
    ),
    Effect.map(Array.getSomes),
  )

~

An edit whose face changed reaches the open chapters of its workspace the way a change on disk does. `reproject` marks them stale and asks Volar to refresh. An edit only to prose leaves the face as it was, and reaches nothing. The other open chapters keep their projections, and Volar keeps the diagnostics it cached for them.

`settleEdits` runs both before the project the server hands to Volar answers a request, while it holds the `reprojecting` semaphore. The rebuilding runs in one synchronous piece, with `runSync`, as the warm-up does. A fiber that paused in the middle of a build would hold the build cache's lock, and a synchronous build in one of Volar's hooks would fail on it. Only the marking waits on a promise, and the marking never touches the build cache.

=>

const settleEdits = (
  server: LanguageServer,
  home: string,
  projects: Cache.Cache<string, LanguageServerProject>,
  edited: Ref.Ref<ReadonlySet<string>>,
): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.sync(() => runtime.runSync(rebuildEdited(server, edited))).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((changes) => reproject(server, home, projects, changes, [])),
  )

## The program

A `ManagedRuntime`, built once from the pipeline layers, warms the services the server runs. The language plugin runs the per-keystroke projection on it, and resolves each file's activated languages from `LoomConfig` on it too. A format request runs `LoomFormatter` on it.

The `onInitialize` handler runs one Effect on that runtime. The program reads the workspace folder the editor opened, falling back to the working directory when the editor names none. It takes `home` to be the workspace that folder belongs to. It finds the tsdk with `tsdkFor` and loads the compiler from it, so a TypeScript section checks against a real standard library.

The program then makes two caches, each keyed by a workspace's root, the `reprojecting` semaphore, whose one permit a reprojection holds while it marks the open looms stale, and the `edited` set of looms the editor has changed since the last request. `services` holds the plugins `loomServicePlugins` collects for a workspace. `projects` holds the TypeScript project `projectOver` builds over those plugins. A cache fills a workspace's entry the first time a request needs it, and fills it once, however many requests ask at the same moment. The program collects the plugins of `home` right away, because Volar announces the server's capabilities from them. The handler registers `workspaceProject`, built over both caches, the semaphore, and the set, and those plugins with Volar. A `MissingTypeScript` failure rejects the handler, and the client receives its message as the error of `initialize`.

A chapter the editor opens can be answered only once the corpus around it is built, and building it is the first and longest wait after the server starts. So the server starts building it before the editor asks. Once the handler has its answer for `initialize`, it schedules `warmCorpus` for `home` a few milliseconds later, so the answer goes out first.

The warm-up runs in one synchronous piece, with `runSync`, never as a background fiber. Volar's hooks run the compiler synchronously too, and a synchronous run cannot wait. A background fiber can pause in the middle of building a module while it holds the build cache's lock. A chapter the editor opened in that moment would find the lock held, and its synchronous build would fail instead of waiting. Run in one piece, the warm-up finishes before the server reads the editor's next message, so a chapter opened meanwhile waits in line and then finds its corpus built. `warmCorpus` reads the workspace's manifest, finds the first loom in the corpus folder the manifest names, and asks the compiler for that loom's reach, which builds the whole corpus into the build cache. When the person opens a chapter a moment later, its corpus is already built, or partly built, and the editor waits only for the rest. A workspace with no manifest, or no loom in its corpus, warms nothing, and a failure only logs, since the editor builds the corpus on demand anyway.

On `initialized`, the server watches every `tsconfig.json` and `jsconfig.json`. Editing a package's compiler options disposes its project, and the next request rebuilds it, so the consumer's options take effect without an editor reload.

=>

const runtime = ManagedRuntime.make(
  Layer.mergeAll(LoomCompiler.layer, LoomFormatter.layer, LoomConfig.layer).pipe(
    Layer.provide(DocumentSource.layer),
    Layer.provide(PackageConfig.layer),
    Layer.provide(LoomConfig.layer),
  ),
)

const warmCorpus = (home: string): Effect.Effect<void, never, LoomCompiler | LoomConfig> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const config = yield* LoomConfig
    const compiler = yield* LoomCompiler
    const manifest = yield* config.manifest(home)
    if (manifest === undefined) return
    const corpus = join(home, manifest.corpus ?? 'corpus')
    const project = Option.some({ root: home, ignore: manifest.ignore ?? [] })
    yield* Option.match(Array.head(loomsIn(corpus, project)), {
      onNone: () => Effect.void,
      onSome: (entry) => Effect.asVoid(compiler.reach(entry)),
    })
  }).pipe(
    Effect.catchCause((cause) => Effect.logWarning('loom: could not warm the corpus', cause)),
  )

export const startLanguageServer = (): void => {
  const connection = createConnection()
  const server = createServer(connection)

  connection.onInitialize(async (params) => {
    const folder = params.workspaceFolders?.[0]?.uri
    const root = folder ? fileURLToPath(folder) : process.cwd()
    const home = workspaceRoot(root)
    const { services, projects, plugins, reprojecting, edited } = await runtime.runPromise(
      Effect.gen(function* () {
        const path = yield* tsdkFor(params.initializationOptions, root)
        const tsdk = yield* Effect.sync(() => loadTsdkByPath(path, params.locale))
        const services = yield* Cache.make({
          capacity: Infinity,
          lookup: (workspace: string) => loomServicePlugins(runtime, tsdk.typescript, workspace),
        })
        const projects = yield* Cache.make({
          capacity: Infinity,
          lookup: (workspace: string) =>
            Cache.get(services, workspace).pipe(
              Effect.flatMap((plugins) => Effect.sync(() => projectOver(server, tsdk, plugins))),
            ),
        })
        const plugins = yield* Cache.get(services, home)
        const reprojecting = yield* Semaphore.make(1)
        const edited = yield* Ref.make<ReadonlySet<string>>(new Set())
        return { services, projects, plugins, reprojecting, edited }
      }),
    )
    setTimeout(() => runtime.runSync(warmCorpus(home)), 10)
    return server.initialize(
      params,
      workspaceProject(server, home, services, projects, reprojecting, edited),
      [...plugins],
    )
  })
  connection.onInitialized(() => {
    server.initialized()
    void server.fileWatcher.watchFiles(['**/tsconfig.json', '**/jsconfig.json'])
  })
  connection.onShutdown(server.shutdown)
  connection.listen()
}

## The server module {Tangle}[src/LoomServer.ts]

The server module assembles the imports, the search for a compiler, the projects kept per workspace, the rereading of closed chapters, and the program, and exports `startLanguageServer`. It also exports `workspaceProject`, `refreshProject`, and `closeOf`, so the cases can drive each one directly. It runs nothing on import — a caller decides when to start the server, so the same module serves both an editor that spawns it and the `loom lsp` command that calls it in-process.

=>

::[Imports]

::[A TypeScript to check against]

::[Every loom answers to its own workspace]

::[A chapter changes behind the editor's back]

::[An edit reaches the other open chapters]

::[The program]

## The run-on-import entry {Tangle}[src/index.ts]

This thin entry starts the server the moment it loads. The VS Code extension bundles it as `server.js`, and the `loom-language-server` bin runs it — both expect loading the module to start the server.

=>

import { startLanguageServer } from './LoomServer'

startLanguageServer()

## Testing a nested workspace

The cases build two workspaces, one nested in the other, the way a Git worktree sits inside the repository it was checked out from. A workspace's store holds a stand-in for the TypeScript service rather than the real one, so the cases need no build and no network. The outer stand-in offers no `format`. The nested one formats by squeezing every double space to one. Both workspaces hold the same loom, whose code is spaced wide.

A stand-in reads Effect from the runtime the host lends, under `runtimeKey`, as a published service does. It declares the version `serviceVersion`, so the loader loads it without a warning. `install` puts a stand-in in a workspace's store, `configure` writes the workspace's `config.yaml`, and `loomAt` writes its loom. `workspaceAt` does all three, and it fills the store before it writes the configuration, in the order `loom add` follows.

=>

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const loomOf = (code: string): string =>
  lines('---', 'Language: TypeScript', '---', '', '# Answer', '', '=>', '', code)

const standIn = (formats: boolean): string =>
  lines(
    `const { Effect } = globalThis.${runtimeKey}.modules.effect`,
    'export default {',
    "  id: 'typescript',",
    "  displayName: 'TypeScript',",
    "  extensions: ['.ts'],",
    '  plugins: () => Effect.succeed([]),',
    ...(formats
      ? ["  format: (request) => Effect.succeed(request.code.replaceAll('  ', ' ')),"]
      : []),
    '}',
  )

const activated = lines('languages:', '  typescript: {}')

const inactive = lines('languages: {}')

const install = (root: string, service: string): void => {
  const store = join(root, '.loom', 'services', 'node_modules', servicePackage('typescript'))
  mkdirSync(store, { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(
    join(store, 'package.json'),
    JSON.stringify({
      name: servicePackage('typescript'),
      version: serviceVersion,
      type: 'module',
      main: 'index.js',
    }),
  )
  writeFileSync(join(store, 'index.js'), service)
}

const configure = (root: string, config: string): void => {
  mkdirSync(join(root, '.loom'), { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(join(root, '.loom', 'config.yaml'), config)
}

const loomAt = (root: string): string => {
  mkdirSync(join(root, 'corpus'), { recursive: true })
  const loom = join(root, 'corpus', 'answer.loom')
  writeFileSync(loom, loomOf('const  answer  =  42'))
  return loom
}

const workspaceAt = (root: string, service: string): string => {
  install(root, service)
  configure(root, activated)
  return loomAt(root)
}

~

The cases start the server from source the way an editor does. The server runs as a process of its own and speaks the Language Server Protocol over its standard input and output. The client hands it the TypeScript this repository installs, as VS Code hands over its own. It leaves `LOOM_HOME` out of the server's environment, since that variable would point every workspace at one store.

The client declares that it watches files for the server, as an editor does. It does not watch any itself. A case reports a file created, changed, or deleted through `changed`, the way an editor's watcher would, and a change is the default. The client answers every request the server makes of it with nothing, whether the server asks for the editor's settings or asks it to watch a file. `open` shows the server a loom, `edit` replaces the text the editor holds for it without saving, under a version that only grows, `shut` closes it without saving, `format` asks for the edits that format it, and `texts` reads the text out of those edits. `diagnostics` asks for the problems the server finds in a loom and returns their messages.

=>

const serverEntry = fileURLToPath(new URL('../src/index.ts', import.meta.url))

const tsdk = dirname(createRequire(import.meta.url).resolve('typescript/lib/typescript.js'))

const editorAt = async (folder: string) => {
  const env = Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(process.env).filter(([key]) => key !== 'LOOM_HOME'))
  const child = spawn(process.execPath, [serverEntry, '--stdio'], {
    env,
    stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'ignore'],
  })
  const connection = createMessageConnection(
    new StreamMessageReader(child.stdout),
    new StreamMessageWriter(child.stdin),
  )
  connection.onRequest(() => null)
  connection.listen()
  await connection.sendRequest('initialize', {
    processId: process.pid,
    rootUri: pathToFileURL(folder).href,
    workspaceFolders: [{ uri: pathToFileURL(folder).href, name: 'outer' }],
    capabilities: { workspace: { didChangeWatchedFiles: { dynamicRegistration: true } } },
    initializationOptions: { typescript: { tsdk } },
  })
  await connection.sendNotification('initialized', {})
  const open = (path: string): Promise<void> =>
    connection.sendNotification('textDocument/didOpen', {
      textDocument: {
        uri: pathToFileURL(path).href,
        languageId: 'loom',
        version: 1,
        text: readFileSync(path, 'utf8'),
      },
    })
  const edit = (path: string, text: string): Promise<void> =>
    connection.sendNotification('textDocument/didChange', {
      textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href, version: Date.now() },
      contentChanges: [{ text }],
    })
  const format = async (path: string): Promise<ReadonlyArray<TextEdit>> =>
    (await connection.sendRequest<TextEdit[] | null>('textDocument/formatting', {
      textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href },
      options: { tabSize: 2, insertSpaces: true },
    })) ?? []
  const diagnostics = async (path: string): Promise<ReadonlyArray<string>> =>
    Array.map(
      (
        await connection.sendRequest<{ readonly items: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> }>(
          'textDocument/diagnostic',
          { textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href } },
        )
      ).items,
      (diagnostic) => diagnostic.message,
    )
  const shut = (path: string): Promise<void> =>
    connection.sendNotification('textDocument/didClose', {
      textDocument: { uri: pathToFileURL(path).href },
    })
  const changed = (path: string, type: FileChangeType = FileChangeType.Changed): Promise<void> =>
    connection.sendNotification('workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles', {
      changes: [{ uri: pathToFileURL(path).href, type }],
    })
  const close = (): void => {
    connection.dispose()
    child.kill()
  }
  return { open, edit, shut, format, diagnostics, changed, close }
}

const texts = (edits: ReadonlyArray<TextEdit>): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  Array.map(edits, (edit) => edit.newText)

~

In the first case the editor opens the outer folder, as it opens a repository, and asks the server to format each loom. The outer loom must come back unchanged, since its workspace offers no formatter for TypeScript. The nested loom must come back with its code squeezed, which only the nested workspace's service can do.

The second case activates a language while the server runs. The nested workspace starts with no language active and nothing in its store, so its loom formats to itself. The case then installs the formatting stand-in and activates TypeScript, as `loom add` would, and reports the changed `config.yaml`. The next format must squeeze the loom's code, with no restart in between.

=>

describe('LoomServer — a loom in a nested workspace', () => {
  it('is formatted by the services of its own workspace', async () => {
    const outer = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const outerLoom = workspaceAt(outer, standIn(false))
    const nestedLoom = workspaceAt(join(outer, 'nested'), standIn(true))
    const editor = await editorAt(outer)
    try {
      await editor.open(outerLoom)
      await editor.open(nestedLoom)
      expect(await editor.format(outerLoom)).toEqual([])
      expect(texts(await editor.format(nestedLoom))).toEqual([loomOf('const answer = 42')])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('is served by a language its workspace activates while the server runs', async () => {
    const outer = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    workspaceAt(outer, standIn(false))
    const nested = join(outer, 'nested')
    configure(nested, inactive)
    const nestedLoom = loomAt(nested)
    const editor = await editorAt(outer)
    try {
      await editor.open(nestedLoom)
      expect(await editor.format(nestedLoom)).toEqual([])
      install(nested, standIn(true))
      configure(nested, activated)
      await editor.changed(join(nested, '.loom', 'config.yaml'))
      expect(texts(await editor.format(nestedLoom))).toEqual([loomOf('const answer = 42')])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing a chapter that changes on disk

A chapter can change while the editor has it closed, and a chapter the editor has open can depend on it. Each case writes its chapters into one workspace. `chapterAt` writes a chapter into the workspace's corpus, and `chapter` is the text of a chapter with one section, under a given title, holding a given line of code. An anchor in this loom's own code would be read as an anchor of this loom, so `anchor` spells one in two pieces.

=>

const chapterAt = (root: string, name: string, text: string): string => {
  mkdirSync(join(root, 'corpus'), { recursive: true })
  const path = join(root, 'corpus', name)
  writeFileSync(path, text)
  return path
}

const chapter = (title: string, code: string): string =>
  lines('---', 'Language: TypeScript', '---', '', `# ${title}`, '', '=>', '', code)

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

~

The first case checks Loom's own diagnostics. A closed chapter, `answer.loom`, holds the section `Answer`, and the open chapter `Main` draws it in with an anchor that names both the section and its chapter. The server finds nothing wrong with `Main`. The case then renames the section in the closed chapter on disk, as a `git checkout` might, and reports the change. `Main` now names a section no chapter holds, and the server must say so.

The next two cases change the set of chapters. In one, `Main` names a chapter that does not exist yet, and the case creates it. In the other, the case deletes the chapter `Main` draws from. Each reports the change. `Main`'s anchor must resolve once the chapter exists, and fail once it is gone.

The fourth case checks the cache Volar keeps for a service that reads no other file, the one the TypeScript service declares. Its workspace activates `checking`, a stand-in TypeScript service that declares the same. On every TypeScript section it reports one diagnostic, whose message is the code of every root the corpus composes, sorted and joined. Asked to count, it also appends how many diagnostics it has reported so far, so a case can tell a fresh check from one Volar kept. A real type check reads another chapter's exports the same way, through the corpus composition.

Each of its three chapters tangles its one section to a file, since the composition holds only the roots that tangle somewhere. The case opens two of them, `main.loom` and `note.loom`, and gives `note.loom` an unsaved edit. A third chapter, `answer.loom`, stays closed. The case rewrites the closed chapter on disk and reports the change. The message on `Main` must now carry the new answer, which only a reprojection of `Main` can bring past Volar's cache. It must still carry the unsaved note, which the server loses if the reprojection rebuilds an open chapter from disk.

The last case closes the edited chapter without saving. The editor reports no change on disk, since none happened, yet the unsaved note now exists nowhere. The message on `Main` must carry the saved note again.

=>

const checking = (loom: string, counted = false): string =>
  lines(
    `const { Effect } = globalThis.${runtimeKey}.modules.effect`,
    'let reported = 0',
    `const { Composition } = globalThis.${runtimeKey}.modules['@athrio/loom-lang-services/LanguageService']`,
    'const composed = (composition) =>',
    `  composition.rootsFor(${JSON.stringify(loom)}).map((file) => file.content.trim()).sort().join(' | ')`,
    'export default {',
    "  id: 'typescript',",
    "  displayName: 'TypeScript',",
    "  extensions: ['.ts'],",
    '  plugins: () => Effect.gen(function* () {',
    '    const composition = yield* Composition',
    '    return [{',
    "      name: 'checking',",
    '      capabilities: { diagnosticProvider: { interFileDependencies: false, workspaceDiagnostics: false } },',
    '      create: () => ({',
    "        provideDiagnostics: (document) => document.languageId === 'typescript'",
    `          ? [{ range: { start: { line: 0, character: 0 }, end: { line: 0, character: 1 } }, message: composed(composition)${counted ? " + ' #' + ++reported" : ''} }]`,
    '          : [],',
    '      }),',
    '    }]',
    '  }),',
    '}',
  )

describe('LoomServer — a chapter that changes on disk', () => {
  it('answers an open chapter from the text a closed one holds now', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const opened = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', `${anchor('Answer')}(answer.loom)`))
    const closed = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer', 'const answer = 42'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(opened)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
      chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Question', 'const answer = 42'))
      await editor.changed(closed)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([
        `Unresolved anchor: no section named \`Answer\`.`,
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('resolves an anchor into a chapter created on disk', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const opened = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', `${anchor('Answer')}(answer.loom)`))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(opened)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([
        `Unresolved anchor: no section named \`Answer\`.`,
      ])
      const created = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer', 'const answer = 42'))
      await editor.changed(created, FileChangeType.Created)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('fails an anchor into a chapter deleted from disk', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const opened = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', `${anchor('Answer')}(answer.loom)`))
    const deleted = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer', 'const answer = 42'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(opened)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
      rmSync(deleted)
      await editor.changed(deleted, FileChangeType.Deleted)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([
        `Unresolved anchor: no section named \`Answer\`.`,
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('checks an open chapter again, keeping the unsaved text of the others', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main))
    configure(root, activated)
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main {Tangle}[main.ts]', 'const main = 1'))
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const closed = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer {Tangle}[answer.ts]', 'const answer = 42'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'unsaved'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(note)).toEqual([
        "const answer = 42 | const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'",
      ])
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual([
        "const answer = 42 | const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'",
      ])
      chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer {Tangle}[answer.ts]', 'const answer = 43'))
      await editor.changed(closed)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual([
        "const answer = 43 | const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'",
      ])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('composes the saved text of a chapter closed without saving', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main))
    configure(root, activated)
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main {Tangle}[main.ts]', 'const main = 1'))
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'unsaved'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(note)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
      await editor.shut(note)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'saved'"])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing an edit that reaches other open chapters

An edit to one open chapter changes what the others read, before it is ever saved. These cases edit open chapters and never touch the disk.

The first case renames a section across two open chapters, the way a rename in the editor does. `Main` draws in the section `Answer` from `answer.loom`. The case renames the section to `Question` in the editor, and renames the anchor in `Main` to match, and saves neither. It then asks only about `Main`. `Main`'s anchor must resolve against the section as the editor holds it, not as the disk still has it.

The second case gives `note.loom` an unsaved edit to its code and asks only about `Main`. The message on `Main` must carry the edited note, which only a reprojection of `Main` can bring past Volar's cache.

The third case checks that an edit only to prose costs the other chapters nothing. Its stand-in counts the diagnostics it reports and appends the count to each message. An edit to the prose of `note.loom` changes nothing `Main` can see, so `Main` keeps the diagnostics Volar cached for it, and the count stays where it was. An edit to the code of `note.loom` then changes what `Main` reads, and `Main` is checked again.

=>

describe('LoomServer — an edit that reaches other open chapters', () => {
  it('resolves an anchor renamed across two open chapters before either is saved', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    configure(root, inactive)
    const opened = chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main', `${anchor('Answer')}(answer.loom)`))
    const answer = chapterAt(root, 'answer.loom', chapter('Answer', 'const answer = 42'))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(opened)
      await editor.open(answer)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
      await editor.edit(answer, chapter('Question', 'const answer = 42'))
      await editor.edit(opened, chapter('Main', `${anchor('Question')}(answer.loom)`))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(opened)).toEqual([])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('checks an open chapter against the unsaved code of another', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main))
    configure(root, activated)
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main {Tangle}[main.ts]', 'const main = 1'))
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'saved'"])
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'unsaved'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)

  it('checks other open chapters again only when an edit changes what they read', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main, true))
    configure(root, activated)
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', chapter('Main {Tangle}[main.ts]', 'const main = 1'))
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'saved' #1"])
      await editor.edit(
        note,
        chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'").replace(
          '[note.ts]\n',
          '[note.ts]\n\nA word of prose.\n',
        ),
      )
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'saved' #1"])
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'edited'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 1 | const note = 'edited' #2"])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing a configuration that changes

A workspace's configuration feeds into what its looms compose. A value warp can read a variable the configuration defines, and the code that anchors the warp composes its value. So a change to a variable changes the composed code of every loom that reads it, with no loom changed at all.

The case defines the variable `answer` in the workspace's configuration. `Main` binds a value warp to it and composes the warp's value into its code. `note.loom` is open too, with an unsaved edit. The case changes the variable on disk and reports the changed configuration. The message on `Main` must carry the new value, and must still carry the unsaved note.

=>

const configuredWith = (answer: number): string =>
  lines('languages:', '  typescript: {}', 'variables:', `  answer: ${answer}`)

const readingAnswer = lines(
  '---',
  'Language: TypeScript',
  '---',
  '',
  '# Main {Tangle}[main.ts]',
  '',
  '{{ answer = ${answer} }}',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  `const main = ${anchor('answer')}`,
)

describe('LoomServer — a configuration that changes', () => {
  it('composes an open chapter under the new configuration, keeping unsaved text', async () => {
    const root = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const main = join(root, 'corpus', 'main.loom')
    install(root, checking(main))
    configure(root, configuredWith(42))
    chapterAt(root, 'main.loom', readingAnswer)
    const note = chapterAt(root, 'note.loom', chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'saved'"))
    const editor = await editorAt(root)
    try {
      await editor.open(main)
      await editor.open(note)
      await editor.edit(note, chapter('Note {Tangle}[note.ts]', "const note = 'unsaved'"))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 42 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
      configure(root, configuredWith(43))
      await editor.changed(join(root, '.loom', 'config.yaml'))
      expect(await editor.diagnostics(main)).toEqual(["const main = 43 | const note = 'unsaved'"])
    } finally {
      editor.close()
    }
  }, 30_000)
})

## Testing the pass piece by piece

Some of what the pass does leaves no trace an editor can see. Volar holds a script in a language service, or holds it nowhere, and no request reveals which. A request that waits on the `reprojecting` semaphore gets the same answer it would have got without waiting. A close that rereads nothing looks the same as one that rereads a file with the same text. So these cases drive the server's parts directly, over stand-ins for Volar's objects.

`recording` is a stand-in for one language service. It holds every script anyone reads from it, and lists every script anyone deletes from it. `projectOf` is a stand-in project that answers every loom from one language service and holds two. `serverWith` is a stand-in server whose editor has the given looms open. The looms sit under a folder in no workspace, so each belongs to `home`.

=>

const recording = () => {
  const scripts = new Map<string, { isAssociationDirty?: boolean }>()
  const deleted: Array<string> = []
  const language = {
    scripts: {
      get: (uri: URI) => {
        const script = scripts.get(uri.toString()) ?? {}
        scripts.set(uri.toString(), script)
        return script
      },
      delete: (uri: URI) => {
        deleted.push(uri.toString())
      },
    },
  }
  return { languageService: { context: { language } }, scripts, deleted }
}

const projectOf = (
  answering: ReturnType<typeof recording>,
  other: ReturnType<typeof recording>,
): LanguageServerProject =>
  ({
    setup: () => {},
    reload: () => {},
    getLanguageService: async () => answering.languageService,
    getExistingLanguageServices: async () => [
      answering.languageService,
      other.languageService,
    ],
  }) as unknown as LanguageServerProject

const serverWith = (...open: ReadonlyArray<URI>): LanguageServer =>
  ({
    documents: { all: () => Array.map(open, (uri) => ({ uri: uri.toString() })) },
  }) as unknown as LanguageServer

const home = '/nowhere'

const mainUri = URI.file('/nowhere/corpus/main.loom')

const goneUri = URI.file('/nowhere/corpus/gone.loom')

~

The first case refreshes a project that holds two language services, the way a project with a `tsconfig.json` does. The editor has `main.loom` open, and the batch deleted `gone.loom`. The open loom must be marked stale in the language service that answers it, and left unread in the other, which would otherwise build a second projection of it. The deleted loom must be forgotten in both, since either may hold its script.

The second case closes looms three ways. A loom whose text matches the file on disk amounts to no change. A loom whose text differs amounts to a change, and a loom whose file is gone amounts to a deletion.

The third case holds the `reprojecting` semaphore, as a reprojection does, and asks the server's project for a loom's language service. The answer must wait until the semaphore is released.

=>

describe('LoomServer — the pass piece by piece', () => {
  it('marks an open loom where it is answered and forgets a deleted one everywhere', async () => {
    const answering = recording()
    const other = recording()
    await Effect.runPromise(
      refreshProject(serverWith(mainUri), home, home, projectOf(answering, other), [goneUri]),
    )
    expect(answering.scripts.get(mainUri.toString())).toEqual({ isAssociationDirty: true })
    expect(other.scripts.has(mainUri.toString())).toBe(false)
    expect(answering.deleted).toEqual([goneUri.toString()])
    expect(other.deleted).toEqual([goneUri.toString()])
  })

  it('turns a close into the change it amounts to', () => {
    const folder = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-server-')))
    const saved = chapterAt(folder, 'saved.loom', chapter('Saved', 'const saved = 1'))
    const uri = URI.file(saved)
    const gone = URI.file(join(folder, 'corpus', 'gone.loom'))
    expect(closeOf(uri, chapter('Saved', 'const saved = 1'))).toEqual([])
    expect(closeOf(uri, chapter('Saved', 'const saved = 2'))).toEqual([
      { uri: uri.toString(), type: FileChangeType.Changed },
    ])
    expect(closeOf(gone, chapter('Gone', 'const gone = 1'))).toEqual([
      { uri: gone.toString(), type: FileChangeType.Deleted },
    ])
  })

  it('holds a request back while a reprojection runs', async () => {
    const answering = recording()
    const { services, projects, reprojecting, edited } = await Effect.runPromise(
      Effect.all({
        services: Cache.make({
          capacity: Infinity,
          lookup: (_: string) => Effect.succeed<ReadonlyArray<LanguageServicePlugin>>([]),
        }),
        projects: Cache.make({
          capacity: Infinity,
          lookup: (_: string) => Effect.succeed(projectOf(answering, recording())),
        }),
        reprojecting: Semaphore.make(1),
        edited: Ref.make<ReadonlySet<string>>(new Set()),
      }),
    )
    await Effect.runPromise(reprojecting.take(1))
    const answer = Promise.resolve(
      workspaceProject(serverWith(), home, services, projects, reprojecting, edited).getLanguageService(
        mainUri,
      ),
    )
    const waited = await Promise.race([
      answer.then(() => 'answered'),
      new Promise<string>((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve('waiting'), 100)),
    ])
    expect(waited).toBe('waiting')
    await Effect.runPromise(reprojecting.release(1))
    expect<unknown>(await answer).toBe(answering.languageService)
  })
})

## What the cases draw on

The cases draw on Bun's test runner, and on Node to make the workspaces, start the server, and turn paths into the `file:` addresses the protocol names documents by. Volar's language-server package carries the client half of the protocol too: `createMessageConnection` and the stream reader and writer it speaks through, the `TextEdit` a format answers with, and the `FileChangeType` a reported change carries. The language store gives `servicePackage` and `serviceVersion`, which name and version the stand-in. The host runtime gives `runtimeKey`, where the stand-in finds Effect. Effect's `Array` reads the text out of the edits a format returns. The cases that drive the server's parts directly import them from the server module, and build the caches, the semaphore, and the set those parts take with Effect's `Cache`, `Semaphore`, and `Ref`, run by `Effect`. They take the types of a server, a project, and a service plugin from Volar, and `URI` names their looms.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import {
  createMessageConnection,
  type Diagnostic,
  FileChangeType,
  StreamMessageReader,
  StreamMessageWriter,
  type TextEdit,
} from '@volar/language-server/node'
import { Array, Cache, Effect, Ref, Semaphore } from 'effect'
import type { LanguageServer, LanguageServerProject, LanguageServicePlugin } from '@volar/language-server/node'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'
import { closeOf, refreshProject, workspaceProject } from '../src/LoomServer'
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process'
import { mkdirSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, realpathSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'
import { servicePackage, serviceVersion } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LoomStore'
import { runtimeKey } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/Runtime'

